### The Wireless Telemetry Echo Sketch

Wire the circuit as shown in Step 2 and upload this sketch. It forwards messages between your smartphone app and your computer's USB Serial Monitor.

```cpp
// Monster Lab - HC-05 Wireless Bluetooth Bridge
#include <SoftwareSerial.h>

// Pin 10 = Arduino RX (connects directly to Module TXD)
// Pin 11 = Arduino TX (connects via voltage divider to Module RXD)
SoftwareSerial btSerial(10, 11);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);   // USB Monitor to Computer
  btSerial.begin(9600); // Standard factory baud rate for HC-05 data mode

  Serial.println("========================================");
  Serial.println("HC-05 WIRELESS BLUETOOTH BRIDGE READY");
  Serial.println("Pair your phone to HC-05 (PIN: 1234).");
  Serial.println("Send text from your terminal app!");
  Serial.println("========================================");
}

void loop() {
  // Forward characters received wirelessly from smartphone to PC monitor
  if (btSerial.available()) {
    char incomingChar = btSerial.read();
    Serial.write(incomingChar);
  }

  // Forward characters typed in PC monitor out wirelessly to smartphone
  if (Serial.available()) {
    char outgoingChar = Serial.read();
    btSerial.write(outgoingChar);
  }
}
```

### Code Breakdown:
1. `btSerial.begin(9600)`: Default factory baud rate for the HC-05 when in normal transparent data mode.
2. When paired, type *'Hello Robot!'* on your phone terminal app—it appears instantly in your Arduino IDE Serial Monitor!
3. Type *'Monster Lab Online'* in your PC Serial Monitor—it pops up on your phone screen over the air!
